3383
05:06:24,344 --> 05:06:30,472
Invoice number 3. Now, when we execute this statement, we get an error. If you look

3384
05:06:30,472 --> 05:06:36,600
at the output window, you will see an error like check option failed. So by default

3385
05:06:36,600 --> 05:06:42,728
when you update data through a view, the modified rows may no longer be included in

3386
05:06:42,728 --> 05:06:48,930
the view. You can use the with check option clause to prevent this. So if you try to

3387
05:06:48,930 --> 05:06:55,132
modify a row in such a way that it would no longer be included in the view, you will

3388
05:06:55,132 --> 05:07:00,374
get an error. As you have seen, views can help us simplify our queries.

3389
05:07:00,374 --> 05:07:04,867
But there are a couple more benefits to using views that I want to briefly talk

3390
05:07:04,867 --> 05:07:09,416
about. The second benefit is that views can reduce the impact of changes to your

3391
05:07:09,416 --> 05:07:14,079
database design. What do I mean by that? Well, imagine you have 10 queries written

3392
05:07:14,079 --> 05:07:18,742
on top of the invoices table. Tomorrow, you decide to make a change in this table.

3393
05:07:18,742 --> 05:07:23,462
You may decide to rename the table or one of its columns. Or you may even decide to

3394
05:07:23,462 --> 05:07:28,239
move one of the columns in this table to a different table. What will happen to your

3395
05:07:28,239 --> 05:07:28,694
queries?

3396
05:07:28,694 --> 05:07:33,496
You'll have to come back and fix all those queries that reference this table. Now,

3397
05:07:33,496 --> 05:07:38,416
what if I told you that you are not allowed to write any queries against this table.

3398
05:07:38,416 --> 05:07:43,043
Instead, you should always use this view that we created earlier, invoices with

3399
05:07:43,043 --> 05:07:47,669
balance. This view looks almost exactly like our invoices table, but it has one

3400
05:07:47,669 --> 05:07:52,530
extra column, the balance, which is also very useful. Now, if we decide to make any

3401
05:07:52,530 --> 05:07:57,274
changes to the underlying table, that is the invoices table, we can simply modify

3402
05:07:57,274 --> 05:07:58,094
our view code,

3403
05:07:58,094 --> 05:08:03,320
and use an alias for the changed columns to bring back their old name. Or if we move

3404
05:08:03,320 --> 05:08:08,483
one of these columns to a different table, we can join the invoices table with that

3405
05:08:08,483 --> 05:08:13,709
table and bring back that column. With this design, the only place we need to change

3406
05:08:13,709 --> 05:08:18,935
is the view. If all our queries are based on this view, they will not be affected by

3407
05:08:18,935 --> 05:08:23,539
a change in the underlying table, the invoices table. So, views provide an

3408
05:08:23,539 --> 05:08:24,534
abstraction over

3409
05:08:24,534 --> 05:08:29,392
are database tables, and this abstraction reduces the impact of changes. There is

3410
05:08:29,392 --> 05:08:34,430
also another benefit to using views. We can use views to restrict access to the data

3411
05:08:34,430 --> 05:08:39,287
in the underlying table. For example, in your view, you may use a where clause to

3412
05:08:39,287 --> 05:08:44,205
filter the records or you may exclude some of the columns in the underlying table.

3413
05:08:44,205 --> 05:08:46,604
If you remove direct access to the table

3414
05:08:46,604 --> 05:08:51,335
users can only update data through your views and they won't be able to modify the

3415
05:08:51,335 --> 05:08:56,123
value of a certain column or modify the rows that are not returned by your view. Of

3416
05:08:56,123 --> 05:09:00,797
course, this is not always that easy and it requires a lot of planning, otherwise

3417
05:09:00,797 --> 05:09:05,412
you will end up with a big mess. So, be aware of these benefits, but don't apply

3418
05:09:05,412 --> 05:09:10,258
them blindly to every situation. Just remember that views can help you simplify your

3419
05:09:10,258 --> 05:09:15,105
queries, so that's a great winner. But also remember that you can use them to put an

3420
05:09:15,105 --> 05:09:16,374
abstraction layer over

3421
05:09:16,374 --> 05:09:22,228
your tables, and reduce the impact of changes. And finally you can use them for data

3422
05:09:22,228 --> 05:09:28,012
security. Alright, we have reached the end of this section, in the next section I'm

3423
05:09:28,012 --> 05:09:33,726
going to talk about stored procedures and functions. At this point you know how to

3424
05:09:33,726 --> 05:09:39,231
write complex queries, you know how to simplify these queries by saving them in

3425
05:09:39,231 --> 05:09:44,318
views, and you also know how to insert, update, and delete data using SQL

3426
05:09:44,318 --> 05:09:49,754
statements. Now, let's say you're building an application that has a database.

3427
05:09:49,754 --> 05:09:54,286
Where are you going to write these SQL statements? You're not going to write them in

3428
05:09:54,286 --> 05:09:58,764
your application code for a couple of reasons. One reason is that it will make your

3429
05:09:58,764 --> 05:10:03,026
application code messy and hard to maintain. For example, if you're building an

3430
05:10:03,026 --> 05:10:07,342
application with C sharp or Java or Python, you don't want to mix your Java code

3431
05:10:07,342 --> 05:10:11,658
with SQL because the SQL code gets in the way and it makes your application code

3432
05:10:11,658 --> 05:10:16,028
look messy and hard to maintain. Also, some programming languages like C sharp or

3433
05:10:16,028 --> 05:10:17,754
Java require a compilation step.

3434
05:10:17,754 --> 05:10:22,493
So if you write your SQL queries in your application code and then find out that you

3435
05:10:22,493 --> 05:10:27,119
need to change one of your queries, you'll have to recompile your application code

3436
05:10:27,119 --> 05:10:31,802
for these changes to take effect. So every time you make a change in your SQL code,

3437
05:10:31,802 --> 05:10:36,541
you will have to recompile and potentially redeploy your application code. For these

3438
05:10:36,541 --> 05:10:41,223
reasons, you should take your SQL code out of your application code and store it in

3439
05:10:41,223 --> 05:10:45,624
a database where it belongs. But where? Inside a stored procedure or function.

3440
05:10:45,624 --> 05:10:51,101
So a stored procedure is a database object that contains a block of SQL code. In our

3441
05:10:51,101 --> 05:10:56,577
application code, we simply call these procedures to get or save the data. So we use

3442
05:10:56,577 --> 05:11:01,793
stored procedures to store and organize our SQL code. But stored procedures have

3443
05:11:01,793 --> 05:11:07,139
other benefits. Most database management systems perform some kind of optimization

3444
05:11:07,139 --> 05:11:12,616
to the code in stored procedures. So the SQL code in stored procedures can sometimes

3445
05:11:12,616 --> 05:11:15,354
be executed faster. Also, just like views,

3446
05:11:15,354 --> 05:11:20,073
stored procedures allow us to enforce data security. For example, we can remove

3447
05:11:20,073 --> 05:11:24,612
direct access to all the tables and allow various operations like inserting,

3448
05:11:24,612 --> 05:11:29,629
updating and deleting data to be performed via stored procedures. Then we can decide

3449
05:11:29,629 --> 05:11:34,467
who can execute which stored procedures. And this will limit what the user can do

3450
05:11:34,467 --> 05:11:39,425
with our data. For example, we can prevent certain users from deleting our data. So

3451
05:11:39,425 --> 05:11:44,024
stored procedures are very powerful and in this section you're going to learn

3452
05:11:44,024 --> 05:11:50,490
how to create and use them. So, let's get started. In this lecture, you're going to

3453
05:11:50,490 --> 05:11:57,033
learn how to create a stored procedure in MySQL. For this demo, we're going to write

3454
05:11:57,033 --> 05:12:03,265
a simple query, like select everything, oops, select everything from the clients

3455
05:12:03,265 --> 05:12:09,498
table. We want to store this query in a stored procedure. We can create a stored

3456
05:12:09,498 --> 05:12:15,963
procedure using the create procedure statement. Then we give our stored procedure a

3457
05:12:15,963 --> 05:12:18,534
name, like get underline clients.

3458
05:12:18,574 --> 05:12:23,948
Note that here I'm only using the lowercase letters and I'm separating multiple

3459
05:12:23,948 --> 05:12:29,663
words using an underscore. This is a common convention amongst MySQL developers. Now

3460
05:12:29,663 --> 05:12:34,901
after the name we add a pair of parentheses. Later I will show you how to add

3461
05:12:34,901 --> 05:12:40,479
parameters here to pass a value to our store procedure. Then we type begin, here's

3462
05:12:40,479 --> 05:12:46,193
our query and then we terminate the store procedure using the end statement. What we

3463
05:12:46,193 --> 05:12:47,894
have in between the begin

3464
05:12:47,894 --> 05:12:52,438
and end keywords is called the body of the store procedure. Now here we only have a

3465
05:12:52,438 --> 05:12:56,873
single statement, but quite often the store procedures that we create in the real

3466
05:12:56,873 --> 05:13:01,308
world have multiple statements. So we'll have to terminate each statement using a

3467
05:13:01,308 --> 05:13:05,908
semicolon, even if we have a single statement here. This is a MySQL requirement, and

3468
05:13:05,908 --> 05:13:10,343
you may not have to worry about this if you use other database management systems

3469
05:13:10,343 --> 05:13:14,832
like SQL Server. Now here we have a couple of syntax errors, don't worry about it,

3470
05:13:14,832 --> 05:13:16,694
we're going to fix it in a second.

3471
05:13:16,694 --> 05:13:21,813
Now here's the tricky part. We want to give all these statements to MySQL as a

3472
05:13:21,813 --> 05:13:27,128
single unit, rather than individual statements separated using a semicolon. So we

3473
05:13:27,128 --> 05:13:32,641
want MySQL to take this entire unit and create a procedure for us called getClients.

3474
05:13:32,641 --> 05:13:37,956
We don't want it to execute individual statements that we have here. How do we do

3475
05:13:37,956 --> 05:13:43,075
this? Well, we'll have to change this default delimiter that is a semicolon to

3476
05:13:43,075 --> 05:13:46,684
