4336
06:31:01,545 --> 06:31:06,585
Either it's committed or rolled back. So with the default locking behavior in MySQL,

4337
06:31:06,585 --> 06:31:11,505
you don't have to worry about concurrency problems most of the time. But there are

4338
06:31:11,505 --> 06:31:15,225
special cases where the default behavior is not sufficient for

4339
06:31:15,225 --> 06:31:20,275
specific scenarios in your applications, in those situations we can overwrite the

4340
06:31:20,275 --> 06:31:25,451
default behavior as I will show you shortly. Next we're going to look at the common

4341
06:31:25,451 --> 06:31:30,564
concurrency problems and how to solve them. Now that you know what concurrency is,

4342
06:31:30,564 --> 06:31:35,801
let's look at the common problems it brings. The first problem is lost updates. This

4343
06:31:35,801 --> 06:31:40,976
happens when two transactions try to update the same data and we don't use logs. In

4344
06:31:40,976 --> 06:31:46,089
this situation, the transaction that commits later will overwrite the changes made

4345
06:31:46,089 --> 06:31:47,835
by the previous transaction.

4346
06:31:47,835 --> 06:31:52,478
For example, let's say that we have two transactions that try to update the same

4347
06:31:52,478 --> 06:31:57,295
customer. One tries to increase the points, the other tries to update the state for

4348
06:31:57,295 --> 06:32:02,055
this customer. Here's our customer record. John in New York with 10 points. Now we

4349
06:32:02,055 --> 06:32:06,408
have two transactions, transaction A that tries to update the state of this

4350
06:32:06,408 --> 06:32:11,283
customer, and transaction B that tries to update the points for this customer. These

4351
06:32:11,283 --> 06:32:16,158
two transactions occur at the same time. This is the customer record that both these

4352
06:32:16,158 --> 06:32:17,145
transactions see.

4353
06:32:17,145 --> 06:32:22,394
Now, let's say transaction A updates the state, but it hasn't committed yet. At the

4354
06:32:22,394 --> 06:32:27,643
same time, transaction B updates the points. Now, the transaction that commits last

4355
06:32:27,643 --> 06:32:32,639
will overwrite the changes made earlier. In this case, if transaction B commits

4356
06:32:32,639 --> 06:32:37,761
last, we will lose the update made by transaction A. How can we prevent this from

4357
06:32:37,761 --> 06:32:42,125
happening? We use logs. So as you saw in the last lecture, by default

4358
06:32:42,125 --> 06:32:47,236
MySQL uses a locking mechanism to prevent two transactions from updating the same

4359
06:32:47,236 --> 06:32:52,411
data at the same time. They will run in sequence one after another, and we'll have

4360
06:32:52,411 --> 06:32:57,396
both updates. Let's look at the next common concurrency problem, dirty reads. A

4361
06:32:57,396 --> 06:33:02,697
dirty read happens when a transaction reads data that hasn't been committed yet. For

4362
06:33:02,697 --> 06:33:07,871
example, transaction A changes the points for a customer from 10 to 20, but before

4363
06:33:07,871 --> 06:33:11,405
it commits the change, transaction B reads this customer

4364
06:33:11,405 --> 06:33:16,901
and based on the customer's points makes a decision. Let's say for every one point,

4365
06:33:16,901 --> 06:33:22,265
it gives a $1 discount to the customer. So it's going to give this customer a $20

4366
06:33:22,265 --> 06:33:27,496
discount. Now, what if transaction A rolls back before transaction B completes?

4367
06:33:27,496 --> 06:33:32,992
Transaction B would have data that had never existed. In other words, this customer

4368
06:33:32,992 --> 06:33:38,555
never had 20 points, this was never committed to the database and transaction B gave

4369
06:33:38,555 --> 06:33:40,475
this customer a $20 discount.

4370
06:33:40,475 --> 06:33:45,487
So in this scenario, we have read uncommitted data in transaction B. Our data was

4371
06:33:45,487 --> 06:33:48,704
dirty. Now to solve this problem, we need to provide

4372
06:33:48,704 --> 06:33:54,019
a level of isolation around our transactions. So data modified by a transaction is

4373
06:33:54,019 --> 06:33:59,335
not immediately visible to other transactions, unless it's committed. The standard

4374
06:33:59,335 --> 06:34:04,456
SQL defines four transaction isolation levels which you're going to learn about

4375
06:34:04,456 --> 06:34:09,382
soon. One of these is read committed. When we use this isolation level for a

4376
06:34:09,382 --> 06:34:14,762
transaction, that transaction can only read committed data. With this we won't have

4377
06:34:14,762 --> 06:34:17,744
dirty reads. Now what if the data gets changed

4378
06:34:17,744 --> 06:34:22,566
after our transaction completes. It doesn't really matter. What matters is that any

4379
06:34:22,566 --> 06:34:27,098
data that we read is committed at the moment it is read. If we make a business

4380
06:34:27,098 --> 06:34:31,862
decision in a transaction, we base our decision on valid, committed data. The data

4381
06:34:31,862 --> 06:34:36,510
can change later after the transaction and our transaction shouldn't worry about

4382
06:34:36,510 --> 06:34:41,158
that. So when we set the isolation level for transaction to read committed, that

4383
06:34:41,158 --> 06:34:45,980
transaction will only read committed data. In the next lecture I'll show you how to

4384
06:34:45,980 --> 06:34:47,374
set the isolation level.

4385
06:34:47,374 --> 06:34:52,338
Now let's look at another concurrency problem. Non-repeatable reads. So by adding

4386
06:34:52,338 --> 06:34:57,301
more isolation to our transaction, we can guarantee that our transaction can only

4387
06:34:57,301 --> 06:35:01,958
read committed data. But what if during the course of a transaction, we read

4388
06:35:01,958 --> 06:35:06,800
something twice and get different results. For example, transaction A reads our

4389
06:35:06,800 --> 06:35:11,579
customer's points, it sees that this customer has 10 points, so it will make a

4390
06:35:11,579 --> 06:35:16,114
business decision based on this value. Now before transaction A completes,

4391
06:35:16,114 --> 06:35:21,353
another transaction, like transaction B, updates the points for this customer to 0.

4392
06:35:21,353 --> 06:35:26,276
Now back to transaction A, we read the points for this customer one more time,

4393
06:35:26,276 --> 06:35:31,326
perhaps as part of a sub query. Now in this transaction, we have read the points

4394
06:35:31,326 --> 06:35:36,375
twice, and each time we have seen a different value. This is a non-repeatable or

4395
06:35:36,375 --> 06:35:41,551
inconsistent read. How should we handle this situation? Well, we can argue that at

4396
06:35:41,551 --> 06:35:42,624
any point in time

4397
06:35:42,624 --> 06:35:47,173
we should make decisions based on what is the most up to date. If that's the case

4398
06:35:47,173 --> 06:35:51,779
for a business scenario, we don't really have to worry about anything here. But we

4399
06:35:51,779 --> 06:35:56,103
can also argue that at the time our transaction started, this customer had 10

4400
06:35:56,103 --> 06:36:00,596
points, so we should have given them a $10 discount. If the points change during

4401
06:36:00,596 --> 06:36:05,258
this transaction, we shouldn't see the changes. We should see the initial snapshot.

4402
06:36:05,258 --> 06:36:09,470
If that's what we want, then we need to increase the isolation level of our

4403
06:36:09,470 --> 06:36:10,144
transaction.

4404
06:36:10,144 --> 06:36:15,178
We need to isolate it from other transactions such that changes to the data aren't

4405
06:36:15,178 --> 06:36:20,274
visible to our transaction. The SQL standard defines another isolation level called

4406
06:36:20,274 --> 06:36:25,370
repeatable reads. With this level, our reads are repeatable and consistent, even if

4407
06:36:25,370 --> 06:36:30,036
the data gets changed by other transactions. We'll see the snapshot that was

4408
06:36:30,036 --> 06:36:35,009
established by the first read. And the last common concurrency problem is phantom

4409
06:36:35,009 --> 06:36:36,974
reads. Imagine in transaction A,

4410
06:36:36,974 --> 06:36:41,894
we're querying all the customers who have more than 10 points. Perhaps we want to

4411
06:36:41,894 --> 06:36:46,874
send them a special discount code. Now at the same time, transaction B updates the

4412
06:36:46,874 --> 06:36:51,915
points for another customer that was not returned by our query. So this customer is

4413
06:36:51,915 --> 06:36:57,017
now eligible for this discount code, but at the time we queried the customers table,

4414
06:36:57,017 --> 06:37:01,937
we didn't see this customer. So after transaction A completes, there is still one

4415
06:37:01,937 --> 06:37:06,674
eligible customer that didn't receive this discount code. This is what we call

4416
06:37:06,674 --> 06:37:11,499
phantom read. Phantom means ghost, so we have data that suddenly appears like a

4417
06:37:11,499 --> 06:37:16,629
ghost and we miss them in our query because they get added, updated or removed after

4418
06:37:16,629 --> 06:37:21,271
we execute our query. How can we solve this problem? Well, it depends on the

4419
06:37:21,271 --> 06:37:26,035
business problem we're trying to solve and how important it is to include this

4420
06:37:26,035 --> 06:37:31,165
customer in our transaction. We can always re-execute transaction A at a later time,

4421
06:37:31,165 --> 06:37:35,074
and this customer will also get a discount code. Not a big deal.

4422
06:37:35,074 --> 06:37:39,503
But if it's absolutely critical to include all eligible customers in our

4423
06:37:39,503 --> 06:37:44,669
transaction, we'll have to make sure that no other transactions are running that can

4424
06:37:44,669 --> 06:37:49,836
impact our query to find the eligible customers. For this, we have another isolation

4425
06:37:49,836 --> 06:37:54,695
level called serializable, and this will guarantee that our transaction will be

4426
06:37:54,695 --> 06:37:59,124
aware of changes currently being made by other transactions to the data.

4427
06:37:59,124 --> 06:38:03,970
If there are other transactions modifying the data that can impact our query result,

4428
06:38:03,970 --> 06:38:08,701
our transaction has to wait for them to complete. So transactions will be executed

4429
06:38:08,701 --> 06:38:13,027
sequentially. This is the highest level of isolation that we can apply to a

4430
06:38:13,027 --> 06:38:17,816
transaction and it gives us the most certainty in our operations. But it comes with

4431
06:38:17,816 --> 06:38:22,431
a cost. The more users and concurrent transactions we have, the more rates we're

4432
06:38:22,431 --> 06:38:27,104
going to experience and our system is going to slow down. So this isolation level

4433
06:38:27,104 --> 06:38:31,919
can hurt performance and scalability. For this reason, we should reserve this only

4434
06:38:31,919 --> 06:38:36,793
in scenarios where it's absolutely critical and necessary to prevent phantom reads.

4435
06:38:36,793 --> 06:38:41,609
Now, if all this sounds overwhelming, don't worry, we're going to review all these

4436
06:38:41,609 --> 06:38:46,541
concurrency problems and how to solve them again. Later in this section, I will show

4437
06:38:46,541 --> 06:38:51,122
you each concurrency problem and how to solve it using a transaction isolation

4438
06:38:51,122 --> 06:38:51,474
level.

4439
06:38:55,184 --> 06:38:59,474
In the last lecture you learned about common concurrency problems and how we can

4440
06:38:59,474 --> 06:39:03,711
prevent them using standard transaction isolation levels. In this lecture we're

4441
06:39:03,711 --> 06:39:07,679
going to review these isolation levels one more time, and I will summarize

4442
06:39:07,679 --> 06:39:12,076
everything for you in a way that you can easily remember. Let's start by reviewing

4443
06:39:12,076 --> 06:39:16,527
the concurrency problems. We have lost updates, which happens when two transactions

4444
06:39:16,527 --> 06:39:20,710
update the same row, and the one that commits last overwrites the changes made

4445
06:39:20,710 --> 06:39:24,464
earlier. We have dirty reads, that happen if we read uncommitted data.

4446
06:39:24,464 --> 06:39:29,526
Another problem is non-repeating reads, which happen if you read the same data twice

4447
06:39:29,526 --> 06:39:34,589
in a transaction, but get different results. And phantom reads, which happen when we

4448
06:39:34,589 --> 06:39:39,350
miss one or more rows in our query, because another transaction is changing the

4449
06:39:39,350 --> 06:39:44,171
data, and we are not aware of the changes in our transaction. We're dealing with

4450
06:39:44,171 --> 06:39:48,691
phantoms or ghosts. Now to solve these four problems, we have four standard

4451
06:39:48,691 --> 06:39:51,524
transaction isolation levels. Read uncommitted,

4452
06:39:51,524 --> 06:39:56,325
which doesn't really protect us from any of these problems because our transactions

4453
06:39:56,325 --> 06:40:00,895
are not isolated from each other. And they can read uncommitted changes by each

4454
06:40:00,895 --> 06:40:05,696
other. Next we have read committed, which gives our transactions a bit of isolation

4455
06:40:05,696 --> 06:40:10,555
so we can only read committed data and this prevents dirty reads. But at this level,

4456
06:40:10,555 --> 06:40:14,951
it is possible that during a transaction, we read something twice and we get

4457
06:40:14,951 --> 06:40:19,637
different results because another transaction updates the data in between the two

4458
06:40:19,637 --> 06:40:19,984
reads.

4459
06:40:19,984 --> 06:40:24,859
So it doesn't really protect us from non-repeatable reads. That brings us to the

4460
06:40:24,859 --> 06:40:29,978
repeatable reads level. At this level we can be confident that multiple reads return

4461
06:40:29,978 --> 06:40:35,036
the same result, even if the data gets changed in the meantime. And finally we have

4462
06:40:35,036 --> 06:40:39,789
serializable, which prevents all these common problems. With this level we can

4463
06:40:39,789 --> 06:40:44,237
prevent phantom reads, so if data is getting changed in the meantime, our

4464
06:40:44,237 --> 06:40:47,284
transaction will wait to get the most recent data.

4465
06:40:47,284 --> 06:40:52,113
Obviously this puts an overhead on the server because it needs extra resources in

4466
06:40:52,113 --> 06:40:57,121
terms of memory and CPU to manage the transactions that have to wait. So the more we

4467
06:40:57,121 --> 06:41:01,950
increase the isolation level, the more performance and scalability problems we're

4468
06:41:01,950 --> 06:41:06,898
going to experience because more locks will be involved to isolate transactions. So

4469
06:41:06,898 --> 06:41:11,549
to recap, a lower isolation level gives us more concurrency, so more users can

4470
06:41:11,549 --> 06:41:16,437
access the same data at the same time. But more concurrency means more concurrency

4471
06:41:16,437 --> 06:41:16,974
problems.

4472
06:41:16,974 --> 06:41:21,987
On the flip side, we achieve a better performance because we need fewer locks to

4473
06:41:21,987 --> 06:41:27,251
isolate transactions from each other. A higher isolation level restricts concurrency

4474
06:41:27,251 --> 06:41:32,452
and that means fewer concurrency problems. But at the cost of decreased performance

4475
06:41:32,452 --> 06:41:37,653
and scalability, because we need more locks and resources. So the fastest isolation

4476
06:41:37,653 --> 06:41:42,855
level is read uncommitted because it doesn't set any locks and it ignores the locks

4477
06:41:42,855 --> 06:41:44,484
set by other transactions.

4478
06:41:44,484 --> 06:41:49,339
For this reason, you may experience all concurrency problems. As we go down this

4479
06:41:49,339 --> 06:41:54,254
list, we get better protection from the concurrency problems, but that also means

4480
06:41:54,254 --> 06:41:58,987
we're going to use more locks, and this requires more resources which can hurt

4481
06:41:58,987 --> 06:42:03,903
performance and scalability. In MySQL, the default transaction isolation level is

4482
06:42:03,903 --> 06:42:09,000
repeatable reads, which works well for most scenarios. It's faster than sterilizable

4483
06:42:09,000 --> 06:42:12,884
and prevents most concurrency problems except phantom reads. So,

4484
06:42:12,884 --> 06:42:17,497
stick to this unless you absolutely have to prevent phantom reads for a specific

4485
06:42:17,497 --> 06:42:22,168
transaction. In that case, you can use the serializable level for that particular

4486
06:42:22,168 --> 06:42:26,493
transaction. As for the other two isolation levels, read committed and read

4487
06:42:26,493 --> 06:42:31,222
uncommitted, you can use this in situations such as bulk reporting where you don't

4488
06:42:31,222 --> 06:42:35,950
need precise consistency or when you deal with data that is rarely updated and you

4489
06:42:35,950 --> 06:42:38,084
want to achieve a better performance.

4490
06:42:38,084 --> 06:42:43,562
Again, I would say stick to the default for most scenarios and only change the

4491
06:42:43,562 --> 06:42:48,689
isolation level if you really have to. Now let me show you how to set the

4492
06:42:48,689 --> 06:42:54,377
transaction isolation level. First, let's view the current isolation level. We're

4493
06:42:54,377 --> 06:42:59,855
going to use the show variables statement to view the system variables used by

4494
06:42:59,855 --> 06:43:05,193
MySQL, and then we're going to filter the list to only see a variable called

4495
06:43:05,193 --> 06:43:08,704
transaction underline isolation. There you go. So,

4496
06:43:08,704 --> 06:43:14,353
the current transaction isolation level is repeatable read. This is the default in

4497
06:43:14,353 --> 06:43:19,864
MySQL. Now to change the transaction isolation level, we use the set transaction

4498
06:43:19,864 --> 06:43:25,375
isolation level statement, and then we type the name of the new isolation level,

4499
06:43:25,375 --> 06:43:31,162
such as serializable. This will set the isolation level for the next transaction. We

4500
06:43:31,162 --> 06:43:36,880
can also set the isolation level for all future transactions in the current session

4501
06:43:36,880 --> 06:43:37,844
or connection.

4502
06:43:37,844 --> 06:43:43,334
So we add the session keyword here. So as long as we have this session or connection

4503
06:43:43,334 --> 06:43:48,628
open, all the future transactions will have this isolation level. We can also set

4504
06:43:48,628 --> 06:43:54,052
the isolation level globally for all new transactions in all sessions. And for that

4505
06:43:54,052 --> 06:43:59,085
we use the global keyword. Now if you're an application developer, you have a

4506
06:43:59,085 --> 06:44:04,444
function or method somewhere in your application that connects to your database to

4507
06:44:04,444 --> 06:44:06,274
execute a given transaction.

4508
06:44:06,274 --> 06:44:10,950
You may use an object relational mapper or directly connect to MySQL. Either way,

4509
06:44:10,950 --> 06:44:15,799
just before executing your transaction, you change the isolation level only for that

4510
06:44:15,799 --> 06:44:20,301
session or connection, and then run your transaction. So you connect to MySQL,

4511
06:44:20,301 --> 06:44:25,092
change the isolation level, execute your transaction and disconnect. This way other

