4730
07:03:23,336 --> 07:03:29,742
All these data types. In MySQL, we have several categories of data types. We have

4731
07:03:29,742 --> 07:03:35,989
string types, numeric types, date and time types, blob types for storing binary

4732
07:03:35,989 --> 07:03:42,553
data, and spatial types for storing geometric or geographical values. Over the next

4733
07:03:42,553 --> 07:03:49,116
few lectures, we're going to explore various data types in each category. So, let's

4734
07:03:49,116 --> 07:03:55,680
get started. In the string category, we have a bunch of data types. The most common

4735
07:03:55,680 --> 07:03:57,736
ones are char and varchar.

4736
07:03:57,736 --> 07:04:02,532
As I told you before, we use chars for storing fixed length strings like the

4737
07:04:02,532 --> 07:04:07,328
abbreviation of states, and varchar for storing variable length strings like

4738
07:04:07,328 --> 07:04:12,250
usernames, passwords, emails, addresses, and so on. Now, even though these are

4739
07:04:12,250 --> 07:04:17,488
string types, we can also use them to store numeric values like zip codes and phone

4740
07:04:17,488 --> 07:04:22,284
numbers, because we don't use these values in mathematical operations. Also,

4741
07:04:22,284 --> 07:04:27,396
sometimes these values may contain a hyphen or parenthesis to group a few digits,

4742
07:04:27,396 --> 07:04:32,295
And that's another reason that we should store these values as a string of

4743
07:04:32,295 --> 07:04:37,393
characters. I personally like to use a standard length for most of my varchar

4744
07:04:37,393 --> 07:04:42,756
columns. For example, I use 50 for short strings like usernames and passwords and

4745
07:04:42,756 --> 07:04:48,185
255 for longer strings like addresses. This consistency simplifies the maintenance

4746
07:04:48,185 --> 07:04:53,548
of your databases. You don't have to constantly check the lengths of each column.

4747
07:04:53,548 --> 07:04:57,256
Now, the maximum length for varchar is just over 65,000.

4748
07:04:57,256 --> 07:05:02,442
So if you store something that is longer, it will get truncated. To prevent that,

4749
07:05:02,442 --> 07:05:07,244
you can use medium text, which allows you to store up to roughly 16 million

4750
07:05:07,244 --> 07:05:12,366
characters. And this is good for storing JSON objects, CSV strings, and short to

4751
07:05:12,366 --> 07:05:17,552
medium length books. We also have long text, which can store up to 4 gigabytes of

4752
07:05:17,552 --> 07:05:22,738
textual data. And this is good for storing textbooks or years of log files. These

4753
07:05:22,738 --> 07:05:27,156
are the main string data types that you'll be using most of the time.

4754
07:05:27,156 --> 07:05:32,536
We also have tiny text, which can store up to 255 characters, and text which can

4755
07:05:32,536 --> 07:05:38,117
store up to 65,000 characters, just like varchar. But for this range of characters,

4756
07:05:38,117 --> 07:05:43,496
it's better to use the varchar type, because it can be indexed. We'll talk about

4757
07:05:43,496 --> 07:05:49,078
indexes later in the course, but basically, we use indexes to speed up our queries.

4758
07:05:49,078 --> 07:05:54,390
Also, all these types support international characters, English letters use one

4759
07:05:54,390 --> 07:05:54,726
byte,

4760
07:05:54,726 --> 07:06:01,541
European and Middle Eastern languages use 2 bytes, and Asian languages like Chinese

4761
07:06:01,541 --> 07:06:07,946
and Japanese use 3 bytes. So if the type of a column is char of 10, MySQL will

4762
07:06:07,946 --> 07:06:14,843
reserve 30 bytes for the values in that column. Next we'll look at integer types. We

4763
07:06:14,843 --> 07:06:21,576
use integers to store whole numbers that don't have a decimal point, like 1, 2, 3,

4764
07:06:21,576 --> 07:06:28,392
4. In MySQL we have 5 integer types, and these are different in terms of the number

4765
07:06:28,392 --> 07:06:30,116
of bytes they use and

4766
07:06:30,116 --> 07:06:36,369
the range of values they can store. Obviously the more bytes we have, the larger the

4767
07:06:36,369 --> 07:06:42,472
numbers we can store. So we have tiny int which takes only one byte, and with this

4768
07:06:42,472 --> 07:06:48,278
we can store the numbers minus 128 to 127. Now if you mark a numeric column as

4769
07:06:48,278 --> 07:06:54,531
unsigned, it can only store positive numbers. So with an unsigned tiny int, we'll be

4770
07:06:54,531 --> 07:06:57,136
able to store the numbers 0 to 255.

4771
07:06:57,136 --> 07:07:02,641
This is particularly useful when storing positive values like people's age. If you

4772
07:07:02,641 --> 07:07:07,475
mark this column as unsigned, we can prevent a negative value from being

4773
07:07:07,475 --> 07:07:13,048
accidentally stored in our database. Next we have small int which takes 2 bytes and

4774
07:07:13,048 --> 07:07:18,688
gives us a bigger range. We also have medium int which takes 3 bytes, int or integer

4775
07:07:18,688 --> 07:07:22,246
which takes 4 bytes, and big int which takes 8 bytes.

4776
07:07:22,246 --> 07:07:27,093
Now don't worry about memorizing any of these ranges, you can always google MySQL

4777
07:07:27,093 --> 07:07:32,119
integer types and you will see this table that clearly shows the number of bytes for

4778
07:07:32,119 --> 07:07:37,026
each type as well as the range of values they can store. Now, if we try to store a

4779
07:07:37,026 --> 07:07:41,813
value outside the range of a columns data type, MySQL throws an error saying the

4780
07:07:41,813 --> 07:07:46,659
value is out of range, so our insert and update operations will fail. Also, apart

4781
07:07:46,659 --> 07:07:51,506
from unsigned, numeric types have another attribute and that is called zero fail.

4782
07:07:51,506 --> 07:07:56,718
This is useful in situations where you want to zero pad the values, so they always

4783
07:07:56,718 --> 07:08:01,930
have the same number of digits. When defining a numeric column, we can specify the

4784
07:08:01,930 --> 07:08:07,014
display size in parenthesis. For example, with int of 4, our numbers will always

4785
07:08:07,014 --> 07:08:12,353
have 4 digits, so the number 1 will be padded with 3 zeros. Note that this will only

4786
07:08:12,353 --> 07:08:17,311
affect how MySQL displays these values, not how it stores them. Now, as a best

4787
07:08:17,311 --> 07:08:21,506
practice, try to use the smallest data type that suits your needs.

4788
07:08:21,506 --> 07:08:26,160
With this, your database will be smaller in size and your queries will execute

4789
07:08:26,160 --> 07:08:31,172
faster. For example, if you want to store the age of people, you can use an unsigned

4790
07:08:31,172 --> 07:08:36,125
tiny int. No one is going to age more than 255 years, at least not anytime soon. So

4791
07:08:36,125 --> 07:08:40,958
one byte is sufficient to store people's age. You may think that saving a byte or

4792
07:08:40,958 --> 07:08:44,836
two doesn't really matter these days because disk space is cheap.

4793
07:08:44,836 --> 07:08:50,283
That is true, but trust me, if you're dealing with large amounts of data, your

4794
07:08:50,283 --> 07:08:56,010
queries will perform better if you use every byte consciously. Because these bytes

4795
07:08:56,010 --> 07:09:01,597
of data will have to be transferred between the disk and the memory. So the less

4796
07:09:01,597 --> 07:09:07,184
data you have in memory, the faster your queries are going to be. So keep things

4797
07:09:07,184 --> 07:09:12,911
smaller and more compact. In MySQL, we have three types for storing numbers with a

4798
07:09:12,911 --> 07:09:18,707
decimal point. We have the decimal type, and this is useful for storing fixed point

4799
07:09:18,707 --> 07:09:19,266
numbers.

4800
07:09:19,266 --> 07:09:24,527
These are the numbers that have a fixed number of digits after the decimal point,

4801
07:09:24,527 --> 07:09:29,917
like monetary values. When using the decimal type, we need to supply two arguments,

4802
07:09:29,917 --> 07:09:35,308
precision and scale. The precision specifies the maximum number of digits, and this

4803
07:09:35,308 --> 07:09:40,763
can be between 1 and 65. The scale determines the number of digits after the decimal

4804
07:09:40,763 --> 07:09:46,218
point. For example, with decimal of 9 and 2, we can store a number with a maximum of

4805
07:09:46,218 --> 07:09:49,206
9 digits, we have 2 digits after, and 7 digits

4806
07:09:49,206 --> 07:09:54,639
before the decimal point. Also, this decimal type has a few synonyms, they are des,

4807
07:09:54,639 --> 07:09:59,941
numeric, and fixed. These are exactly the same. So just stick to decimal and keep

4808
07:09:59,941 --> 07:10:05,111
things consistent. I mentioned these synonyms in case you see them in databases

4809
07:10:05,111 --> 07:10:10,479
designed by others. We also have the double and float types, and these are used in

4810
07:10:10,479 --> 07:10:15,977
scientific calculations. If you want to perform calculations with very large or very

4811
07:10:15,977 --> 07:10:18,726
small numbers, you should use these types.

4812
07:10:18,726 --> 07:10:23,375
Just remember, these types don't store the exact value, they use an approximation.

4813
07:10:23,375 --> 07:10:28,137
That's the reason they allow us to store really large or really small numbers. Float

4814
07:10:28,137 --> 07:10:32,729
takes 4 bytes, double takes 8 bytes, so obviously with double we can store larger

4815
07:10:32,729 --> 07:10:37,378
numbers. So if you want to work with exact values like monetary values, you should

4816
07:10:37,378 --> 07:10:41,857
use the decimal type. If you want to work with really large or small numbers in

4817
07:10:41,857 --> 07:10:46,619
scientific calculations, and the precision is not important, you should use float or

4818
07:10:46,619 --> 07:10:47,016
double.

4819
07:10:52,516 --> 07:10:57,510
There are times that we need to store a yes or no kind of value. For example, is

4820
07:10:57,510 --> 07:11:02,566
this blog post published or not? That's when we use boolean values which are true

4821
07:11:02,566 --> 07:11:07,809
and false. In my skill we have a data type called bool or boolean and this data type

4822
07:11:07,809 --> 07:11:12,990
is actually a synonym for tiny int. So with a boolean column we can write code like

4823
07:11:12,990 --> 07:11:17,922
this. Now this true keyword you see here is internally represented as a one and

