we specify the list of allowable values, and then we can store multiple values in a

4870
07:14:59,775 --> 07:15:04,152
column. Again, this is really bad because it causes all sorts of issues.

4871
07:15:04,152 --> 07:15:10,004
In the next section, when we talk about normalization, you will learn a better

4872
07:15:10,004 --> 07:15:16,231
approach to deal with these scenarios. In MySQL we have four data types for storing

4873
07:15:16,231 --> 07:15:22,532
date and time values. We have date for storing a date without a time component, time

4874
07:15:22,532 --> 07:15:28,609
for storing a time value, date time, timestamp, and year for storing a four digit

4875
07:15:28,609 --> 07:15:34,761
year. We often use timestamps for keeping track of when a row was inserted or last

4876
07:15:34,761 --> 07:15:38,362
updated. We can also use the date time for that.

4877
07:15:38,362 --> 07:15:45,015
The difference is that timestamp is 4 bytes and it can only store dates up to the

4878
07:15:45,015 --> 07:15:51,750
year 2038. This is called the year 2038 problem. Date time uses 8 bytes, so if you

4879
07:15:51,750 --> 07:15:58,649
want to store dates that go beyond the year 2038, you should use the date time type.

4880
07:15:58,649 --> 07:16:05,384
We use blob types to store large amounts of binary data like images, videos, pdfs,

4881
07:16:05,384 --> 07:16:10,312
word files, pretty much any binary data. In my skill we have

4882
07:16:10,312 --> 07:16:15,936
four blob types, and they differ based on the maximum amount of data they can store.

4883
07:16:15,936 --> 07:16:21,426
We have tiny blob for storing binary data up to 255 bytes, blob for storing binary

4884
07:16:21,426 --> 07:16:27,050
data up to 65 kilobytes, medium blob for storing binary data up to 16 megabytes, and

4885
07:16:27,050 --> 07:16:32,674
long blob for storing binary data up to 4 gigabytes. Generally speaking, it's better

4886
07:16:32,674 --> 07:16:38,231
to keep your files out of your databases, because relational databases are designed

4887
07:16:38,231 --> 07:16:39,302
for working with

4888
07:16:39,302 --> 07:16:44,305
structured relational data, not binary data. If you store your files in a database,

4889
07:16:44,305 --> 07:16:49,308
your database size is going to increase pretty quickly and that will slow down your

4890
07:16:49,308 --> 07:16:54,372
backups. You will also experience performance problems because pulling images out of

4891
07:16:54,372 --> 07:16:59,435
your database is always slower than reading them from the file system. Plus you will

4892
07:16:59,435 --> 07:17:04,258
have to write additional code to read or store images in a database. If you have

4893
07:17:04,258 --> 07:17:07,332
valid reasons for storing your files in a database,

4894
07:17:07,332 --> 07:17:13,341
First, take all this into account and make sure these reasons don't apply to your

4895
07:17:13,341 --> 07:17:19,423
scenario. In MySQL, we can also store JSON documents. In case you are not familiar

4896
07:17:19,423 --> 07:17:25,358
with JSON or JavaScript object notation, it's basically a lightweight format for

4897
07:17:25,358 --> 07:17:31,589
storing and transferring data over the internet. It's used heavily in web and mobile

4898
07:17:31,589 --> 07:17:37,597
applications, so most of the time, your mobile apps send your data to the backend

4899
07:17:37,597 --> 07:17:42,122
using JSON. In JSON, we use curly braces to define an object.

4900
07:17:42,122 --> 07:17:47,145
Inside these braces we can have one or more key value pairs. Keys are always

4901
07:17:47,145 --> 07:17:52,564
strings, so we need to surround them with quotes. Values can be anything, they can

4902
07:17:52,564 --> 07:17:57,586
be strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, or other objects. So in our SQL store

4903
07:17:57,586 --> 07:18:03,005
database, let's open up the products table, and add a new column here. We're going

4904
07:18:03,005 --> 07:18:07,962
to call that properties, to store additional properties about each product.

