07:29:00,711 --> 07:29:06,057
well-structured databases. Are you ready? Let's get started. In this lecture, we're

5027
07:29:06,057 --> 07:29:10,952
going to talk about data modeling, which is the process of creating a model.

5028
07:29:10,952 --> 07:29:15,869
For the data that we want to store in a database. It involves four steps. The first

5029
07:29:15,869 --> 07:29:20,785
step is to understand and analyze the business requirements. Unfortunately, so many

5030
07:29:20,785 --> 07:29:25,465
developers skip this step and jump into creating tables in a database. But this

5031
07:29:25,465 --> 07:29:30,322
step, in my opinion, is the most important step in the data modeling process. Yes,

5032
07:29:30,322 --> 07:29:35,238
it's a bit slow and takes a bit of time, but the better you understand the business

5033
07:29:35,238 --> 07:29:40,155
problem you're trying to solve, the better you can come up with a solution, in this

5034
07:29:40,155 --> 07:29:41,162
case, a database.

5035
07:29:41,162 --> 07:29:46,117
So before you think about your tables and columns, you need to fully understand the

5036
07:29:46,117 --> 07:29:50,654
business requirements. You need to talk to the business stakeholders, domain

5037
07:29:50,654 --> 07:29:55,490
experts, and even end users. Look at the existing forms, documents, applications,

5038
07:29:55,490 --> 07:30:00,504
spreadsheets, databases, pretty much anything that is relevant to the problem domain

5039
07:30:00,504 --> 07:30:05,519
you're working on. Once you collect all this information, your next step is to build

5040
07:30:05,519 --> 07:30:10,355
a conceptual model of the business. And this involves identifying the entities or

5041
07:30:10,355 --> 07:30:10,892
things or

5042
07:30:10,892 --> 07:30:16,092
concepts in the business and their relationships with each other. A conceptual model

5043
07:30:16,092 --> 07:30:21,167
is just a visual representation of these concepts that you use to communicate with

5044
07:30:21,167 --> 07:30:25,748
the stakeholders so you know you're both on the same page. Once you have a

5045
07:30:25,748 --> 07:30:30,824
conceptual model, you refine it to come up with a data model or data structure for

5046
07:30:30,824 --> 07:30:35,899
storing data. This is what we call a logical model. A logical model is an abstract

5047
07:30:35,899 --> 07:30:39,242
data model that is independent of database technology.

5048
07:30:39,242 --> 07:30:43,979
It just shows you the tables and columns you need. Next, you refine this logical

5049
07:30:43,979 --> 07:30:48,658
model to build a physical model for a specific database management system. So a

5050
07:30:48,658 --> 07:30:53,454
physical model is the implementation of a logical model for a particular database

5051
07:30:53,454 --> 07:30:58,192
technology. In this model you should have the exact data types supported by your

5052
07:30:58,192 --> 07:31:03,047
database technology, the default values for your columns, whether they're nullable

5053
07:31:03,047 --> 07:31:03,462
or not,

5054
07:31:03,462 --> 07:31:08,707
the primary keys for your tables, as well as other objects like views, stored

5055
07:31:08,707 --> 07:31:14,089
procedures, triggers, and so on. So a physical data model is very specific to a

5056
07:31:14,089 --> 07:31:19,743
database technology like MySQL. So this is the process of data modeling. Now, don't

5057
07:31:19,743 --> 07:31:25,192
worry if all this sounds a little bit ambiguous. Over the next few lectures, I'm

