5126
07:36:41,954 --> 07:36:47,875
So, what are we going to have in this logical model? Well, first we need to specify

5127
07:36:47,875 --> 07:36:53,368
the type of each attribute, like string, integer, float, date, and so on. For

5128
07:36:53,368 --> 07:36:59,361
example, the student's name is a string, so here in parenthesis we type string. Note

5129
07:36:59,361 --> 07:37:04,854
that here we have a string, not varchar, because varchar is an implementation

5130
07:37:04,854 --> 07:37:10,847
detail. It's a data type in MySQL. But this logical model is independent of database

5131
07:37:10,847 --> 07:37:11,774
technologies.

5132
07:37:11,774 --> 07:37:17,130
Now similarly email is a string, so let's add that here as well. And date registered

5133
07:37:17,130 --> 07:37:22,422
is a date time. Now it's often a good practice to split an attribute like name into

5134
07:37:22,422 --> 07:37:27,713
multiple attributes like first name and last name. With this we can easily query or

5135
07:37:27,713 --> 07:37:32,559
sort students by their first or last name. In contrast, if you have a single

5136
07:37:32,559 --> 07:37:37,851
attribute to store the full name, then we'll have to deal with extracting the first

5137
07:37:37,851 --> 07:37:41,804
or last name, and this will make our queries complex and slow.

5138
07:37:41,804 --> 07:37:47,138
The same is true when you have an address attribute. You should break it into

5139
07:37:47,138 --> 07:37:52,679
smaller pieces like street, city, zip code, country, and so on. This way you can

5140
07:37:52,679 --> 07:37:58,360
query on these individual pieces. So let's split the name attribute into first and

5141
07:37:58,360 --> 07:38:03,901
last names. That is better. Next we need to specify the type of the relationship

5142
07:38:03,901 --> 07:38:09,651
between our entities. We have three main types of relationships. One to one, one to

5143
07:38:09,651 --> 07:38:11,244
many, and many to many.

5144
07:38:11,244 --> 07:38:15,845
The other types are variations of these three types. In this case we have a many to

5145
07:38:15,845 --> 07:38:20,390
many relationship between the student and the course, because a student can enroll

5146
07:38:20,390 --> 07:38:24,935
in multiple courses, and the course can have multiple students. Now if you want to

5147
07:38:24,935 --> 07:38:29,536
change the type of this relationship, we can select it, and here on the right side,

5148
07:38:29,536 --> 07:38:33,693
we have this panel, from this drop down list, we can select the type of the

5149
07:38:33,693 --> 07:38:38,349
relationship. Now you see a lot of different shapes here, because draw.io supports a

5150
07:38:38,349 --> 07:38:41,564
lot of different visual languages, some of these icons are

5151
07:38:41,564 --> 07:38:46,358
part of UML. So down the bottom you can see the icons for entity relationship

5152
07:38:46,358 --> 07:38:51,463
diagrams. If this is confusing for you, you can also hover your mouse over each of

5153
07:38:51,463 --> 07:38:56,444
these relationships, so this is a one to one relationship, here is a one to many

5154
07:38:56,444 --> 07:39:01,674
relationship and so on. So in this logical model, we should clearly specify the type

5155
07:39:01,674 --> 07:39:06,779
of relationship between our entities. At the conceptual level this doesn't matter,

5156
07:39:06,779 --> 07:39:11,324
all we need is a straight line between entities, the type doesn't matter.

5157
07:39:11,514 --> 07:39:15,746
Now, there is something missing here. What if we need to know the date a student

5158
07:39:15,746 --> 07:39:20,136
enrolled in a course? Where should we put that date attribute? We can't put it here

5159
07:39:20,136 --> 07:39:24,315
on the student, because a student may enroll in several courses. Then we need a

5160
07:39:24,315 --> 07:39:28,759
separate attribute for each course, and we won't know ahead of time how many courses

5161
07:39:28,759 --> 07:39:33,044
each student is going to enroll in. That doesn't make sense. What if we put it on

5162
07:39:33,044 --> 07:39:37,487
the course entity? That doesn't make sense either, because the enrollment date would

5163
07:39:37,487 --> 07:39:39,074
be different for each student.

5164
07:39:39,074 --> 07:39:44,643
So the enrollment date is not an attribute of the course. It's an attribute of the

5165
07:39:44,643 --> 07:39:50,281
enrollment, right? So it's an attribute of the relationship between the student and

5166
07:39:50,281 --> 07:39:55,918
the course. This is a common scenario in the real world that a lot of data modelers

5167
07:39:55,918 --> 07:40:01,216
don't get right. So here we need to introduce a new entity that represents the

5168
07:40:01,216 --> 07:40:06,853
relationship between the student and the course. Let me add that here and rename it

5169
07:40:06,853 --> 07:40:07,804
to enrollment.

5170
07:40:08,314 --> 07:40:13,822
Now in this entity we're going to have an attribute called date. And the type of

5171
07:40:13,822 --> 07:40:19,468
this attribute is date time. Now we need to delete this relationship between these

5172
07:40:19,468 --> 07:40:25,183
two entities and recreate it through this new entity. So select this, delete. Now a

5173
07:40:25,183 --> 07:40:30,898
student can have many enrollments, but each enrollment is for a particular student.

5174
07:40:30,898 --> 07:40:36,544
So here we have a one to many relationship between the student and the enrollment.

5175
07:40:37,054 --> 07:40:43,005
So I'm going to pick this relationship on the left side, add it here. Now let's drag

5176
07:40:43,005 --> 07:40:48,459
and drop it on the student and the enrollment. So a student can have multiple

5177
07:40:48,459 --> 07:40:54,126
enrollments and that is why we have this fork icon on this side. This represents

5178
07:40:54,126 --> 07:40:59,935
many, right? Similarly a course can have multiple enrollments, but each enrollment

5179
07:40:59,935 --> 07:41:05,319
is for a particular course. So once again we need to add another one to many

5180
07:41:05,319 --> 07:41:06,594
relationship here.

5181
07:41:06,594 --> 07:41:12,394
Let's move this around. So I'm going to attach this end to the course entity and

5182
07:41:12,394 --> 07:41:18,338
this other end to the enrollment. So one too many. So previously we had a many too

5183
07:41:18,338 --> 07:41:23,703
many relationship between these two entities, now we have two one too many

5184
07:41:23,703 --> 07:41:29,792
relationships. You see that a lot in modeling relational databases. So now we have a

5185
07:41:29,792 --> 07:41:35,447
new entity that clearly represents an enrollment for a student in a particular

5186
07:41:35,447 --> 07:41:35,954
course.

5187
07:41:35,954 --> 07:41:40,741
In this entity currently we only have one attribute that's the date, but let's say

5188
07:41:40,741 --> 07:41:45,645
we talk to our domain expert and we realize that courses are priced differently over

5189
07:41:45,645 --> 07:41:50,316
time. So each student may buy a course at a different price. Where should we put

5190
07:41:50,316 --> 07:41:55,103
that price? In the enrollment entity. That is the price a student pays at the time

5191
07:41:55,103 --> 07:41:59,949
of enrolling in a course. So, let's add another attribute here, price, and the type

5192
07:41:59,949 --> 07:42:04,094
of this should be float because we can have digits after decimal point.

5193
07:42:04,094 --> 07:42:08,746
Like $49.50. Once again, at this level, we don't care what is the name of the

5194
07:42:08,746 --> 07:42:13,639
corresponding data type in MySQL or any other database management systems. That's

5195
07:42:13,639 --> 07:42:18,411
why we use float. Now in MySQL, we're going to use decimal. In another database

5196
07:42:18,411 --> 07:42:23,365
management system, we might have a different type. Our logical model doesn't care.

5197
07:42:23,365 --> 07:42:28,258
This is just an abstract data structure. So compare our conceptual model with our

5198
07:42:28,258 --> 07:42:33,212
logical model. Our conceptual model doesn't really give us a structure for storing

5199
07:42:33,212 --> 07:42:33,514
data.

5200
07:42:33,514 --> 07:42:38,447
It only represents the business entities and their relationship. And we use it to

5201
07:42:38,447 --> 07:42:43,441
understand the problem domain and communicate with the domain experts. Our logical

5202
07:42:43,441 --> 07:42:48,009
model adds more details to our conceptual model, so now we almost know what

5203
07:42:48,009 --> 07:42:53,003
structure or what tables we need to store our data. The entities that we have here

5204
07:42:53,003 --> 07:42:57,875
eventually end up as tables in our database. Alright, now let's add the type for

5205
07:42:57,875 --> 07:43:02,869
these attributes as well. So title is going to be a string, price is going to be a

5206
07:43:02,869 --> 07:43:03,234
float,

5207
07:43:03,614 --> 07:43:09,599
instructor is going to be also a string, and tags, we can keep things simple, so we

5208
07:43:09,599 --> 07:43:15,440
can use a string, and if a course has multiple tags, we can separate them using a

5209
07:43:15,440 --> 07:43:21,497
comma. Now you might say, Mosh, this is not a good idea, I know, we're going to talk

5210
07:43:21,497 --> 07:43:27,410
about it later when we get to normalization. So for now, let's keep things simple.

5211
07:43:27,410 --> 07:43:33,467
Next we're going to take this logical model and implement it in MySQL. As I told you

5212
07:43:33,467 --> 07:43:34,044
earlier,

5213
07:43:34,044 --> 07:43:39,603
a physical model is the implementation of a logical model for a specific database

5214
07:43:39,603 --> 07:43:44,956
technology. So in this lecture, we're going to take our logical data model and

5215
07:43:44,956 --> 07:43:50,377
create a physical data model for MySQL. So here in MySQL Workbench, on the top,

5216
07:43:50,377 --> 07:43:55,936
let's go to the file menu and select new model. Now this EER that you see here is

5217
07:43:55,936 --> 07:44:00,946
short for enhanced entity relationship. So here we can also create entity

5218
07:44:00,946 --> 07:44:03,554
relationship diagrams. Now by default,

5219
07:44:03,554 --> 07:44:09,681
this model assumes a database called mydb, we can right click this and go to edit

5220
07:44:09,681 --> 07:44:15,808
schema to rename it. So, let's change the name of this database to school. Now we

5221
07:44:15,808 --> 07:44:21,935
haven't created a database yet, this is just a model or a physical model for that

5222
07:44:21,935 --> 07:44:28,289
database, okay? Now, on the top, let's add a new diagram. In this diagram we can add

5223
07:44:28,289 --> 07:44:34,416
tables and views. So here's the icon for adding a new table, let's click this and

5224
07:44:34,416 --> 07:44:35,324
add it here.

5225
07:44:35,324 --> 07:44:40,231
Now let's double click this and rename it to something meaningful. In this case,

5226
07:44:40,231 --> 07:44:45,077
students. Now when it comes to naming tables, I personally prefer to use plural

5227
07:44:45,077 --> 07:44:49,862
names, because a table is a container for several entities like students. Some

5228
07:44:49,862 --> 07:44:54,953
people prefer to use singular names for their tables, and they have their own valid

5229
07:44:54,953 --> 07:45:00,106
reasons. Whatever you prefer, go for it, but stick with that convention. If there is

5230
07:45:00,106 --> 07:45:04,154
already a convention in place, follow that. Don't break the rules.

5231
07:45:04,154 --> 07:45:08,794
It's much better to stick to the existing convention, even if it's not ideal,

5232
07:45:08,794 --> 07:45:13,856
otherwise your development and maintenance cost is going to increase pretty quickly.

5233
07:45:13,856 --> 07:45:18,616
You have to constantly remember if a table name is singular or plural. So in my

5234
07:45:18,616 --> 07:45:23,557
logical models, I use singular names for my entities, and in my physical models, I

5235
07:45:23,557 --> 07:45:28,498
use plural names for my tables. Now let's add columns to this table. First, let me

5236
07:45:28,498 --> 07:45:32,114
zoom in a little bit so you can see clearly, that is better.

5237
07:45:32,114 --> 07:45:37,533
Now I'm going to close the navigator panel, so here's our table designer, here we

5238
07:45:37,533 --> 07:45:42,751
can add the columns in this table. Now back to our logical model, here are the

5239
07:45:42,751 --> 07:45:48,303
columns that we need to add, first name, last name, email, and date registered. So,

5240
07:45:48,303 --> 07:45:53,923
first I'm going to add the first name, first underline name, so here I'm following a

5241
07:45:53,923 --> 07:45:59,409
different convention, that is a MySQL convention, we separate multiple words using

5242
07:45:59,409 --> 07:46:03,824
an underscore. Now the type of this column is going to be varchar,

5243
07:46:03,824 --> 07:46:09,288
of let's say 50. So as I told you before, I use standard length for my strings, so

5244
07:46:09,288 --> 07:46:14,685
for shorter strings like username, password, first name, I use 50, and for medium

5245
07:46:14,685 --> 07:46:20,082
length strings I use 255. This is just my personal preference. Now you can see by

5246
07:46:20,082 --> 07:46:25,412
default, MySQL marks this column as a primary key, we don't want this to happen.

5247
07:46:25,412 --> 07:46:30,809
We're going to talk about primary keys later. So for now, let's uncheck this box.

5248
07:46:30,809 --> 07:46:33,674
Now here this column is marked as not null,

5249
07:46:33,674 --> 07:46:40,116
So we cannot accept null values in this column. That's good. Now let's add a second

5250
07:46:40,116 --> 07:46:46,403
column. Last name, also varchar of 50. We want to mark this column as not null as

5251
07:46:46,403 --> 07:46:52,768
well. Now let's add the email, also varchar of maybe 50 is not enough here because

5252
07:46:52,768 --> 07:46:59,287
perhaps some people's email is longer. So I'm going to go with 255. And also we want

5253
07:46:59,287 --> 07:47:03,634
to mark this as not null. And finally we're going to add

5254
07:47:03,634 --> 07:47:09,319
date, underline, registered. And for this we're going to use a date time, so we can

5255
07:47:09,319 --> 07:47:14,936
store both the date and the time the student registered. Also we want to mark this

5256
07:47:14,936 --> 07:47:20,484
as not null. So at this level, in this physical model, we have the exact types in

5257
07:47:20,484 --> 07:47:25,895
MySQL, we also know if columns are nullable or not. We can also set the default

5258
07:47:25,895 --> 07:47:31,580
values, this is the difference between a physical model and a logical model that is

5259
07:47:31,580 --> 07:47:34,114
independent of database technologies.

5260
07:47:34,114 --> 07:47:40,313
So, here's the end result, this is our students table, now it's your turn. Add the

5261
07:47:40,313 --> 07:47:46,588
other entities that we define in our logical model to this physical model. For now,

5262
07:47:46,588 --> 07:47:52,938
don't worry about their relationships, just add the tables and their columns. You'll

5263
07:47:52,938 --> 07:47:58,759
see my solution next. Alright, here's my solution. So we have two new tables,

5264
07:47:58,759 --> 07:48:05,034
courses and enrollments. In the courses table we have title of type varchar of 255.

5265
07:48:05,034 --> 07:48:10,383
For the price I have used decimal of 5 and 2, and that means we can have a maximum

5266
07:48:10,383 --> 07:48:15,797
of 5 digits with 2 digits after the decimal point. So the maximum price that we can

5267
07:48:15,797 --> 07:48:21,081
allow here is $999. Now how did they come up with this? Let's say I talked to the

5268
07:48:21,081 --> 07:48:26,495
business stakeholders, so they told me that the maximum price they want to allow on

5269
07:48:26,495 --> 07:48:31,779
their website is $999. Now if this rule changes in the future, we can always come

5270
07:48:31,779 --> 07:48:33,214
back and fix this. So,

5271
07:48:33,214 --> 07:48:38,786
I didn't go for a decimal of 9 and 2 right from the get go because that would waste

5272
07:48:38,786 --> 07:48:44,224
our disk space. As I told you before, keep things small. Next we have instructor,

5273
07:48:44,224 --> 07:48:49,863
for the instructor I have used varchar of 255, we could go less, like 50 characters,

5274
07:48:49,863 --> 07:48:55,435
but I just wanted to be on the safe side, so that's why I used 255 characters here.

5275
07:48:55,435 --> 07:48:59,194
And also for tags I have used varchar of 255 characters.

5276
07:48:59,194 --> 07:49:04,418
Now all these columns are required, so we do not accept null values. Here we have

5277
07:49:04,418 --> 07:49:09,578
the enrollments table, with two columns, date and price. Note that I have called

5278
07:49:09,578 --> 07:49:14,867
this column date, not enrollment date, because this is a little bit repetitive and

5279
07:49:14,867 --> 07:49:19,963
redundant. I've seen a lot of data modelers prefixing their column names with a

5280
07:49:19,963 --> 07:49:25,187
table name, like enrollment date and enrollment price. This is really unnecessary

5281
07:49:25,187 --> 07:49:28,154
and makes your code ugly. Date is an attribute

5282
07:49:28,154 --> 07:49:34,143
of enrollment, not enrollment date. So, just a little side note, now the type of

5283
07:49:34,143 --> 07:49:39,833
this column is date time, so we can store both the date and the time someone

5284
07:49:39,833 --> 07:49:46,046
enrolled in a course. And the price column in this table is also a decimal of 5 and

5285
07:49:46,046 --> 07:49:52,035
2, so look at the consistency between these tables. Alright, we're not done yet,

5286
07:49:52,035 --> 07:49:58,324
next we need to add a primary key to each table and define the relationships between

5287
07:49:58,324 --> 07:50:03,564
these tables. Alright, now we need to add a primary key to each table.

5288
07:50:03,564 --> 07:50:08,422
As you know, a primary key is a column that uniquely identifies each record in a

5289
07:50:08,422 --> 07:50:13,401
given table. So, what column can we use here to uniquely identify each student? We

5290
07:50:13,401 --> 07:50:18,501
cannot use the first name because many people can have the same first name. The same

5291
07:50:18,501 --> 07:50:23,480
is true for the last name. What about the combination of the first and last names?

5292
07:50:23,480 --> 07:50:28,399
This is what we call a composite primary key. A composite primary key is one that

5293
07:50:28,399 --> 07:50:29,674
has multiple columns.

5294
07:50:29,674 --> 07:50:34,815
Again, here we cannot rely on the combination of the first and last names, because

5295
07:50:34,815 --> 07:50:39,893
two people can have the same full name. John Smith and John Smith. What about the

5296
07:50:39,893 --> 07:50:45,097
email? Well, email certainly uniquely identifies each student, but the problem with

5297
07:50:45,097 --> 07:50:50,112
the email is that it can be several characters long. So as you will see shortly,

5298
07:50:50,112 --> 07:50:55,378
when we add a relationship between these two tables, this primary key in this table,

5299
07:50:55,378 --> 07:50:58,074
in this case email, is going to be repeated

5300
07:50:58,074 --> 07:51:03,085
in the enrollments table. So every time a student enrolls in a course, his primary

5301
07:51:03,085 --> 07:51:08,219
key, in this case email, is going to get repeated in the enrollments table. And that

5302
07:51:08,219 --> 07:51:13,047
is not a good idea, because we're repeating several characters. Also, ideally a

5303
07:51:13,047 --> 07:51:17,997
primary key should not change. So its value should stay the same forever. So this

5304
07:51:17,997 --> 07:51:23,131
email column is not a good candidate for a primary key, because a student can change

5305
07:51:23,131 --> 07:51:24,414
their email later on.

5306
07:51:24,414 --> 07:51:30,474
So in situations like this, we introduce a new column, which we call an ID column,

5307
07:51:30,474 --> 07:51:36,460
and we use that as the primary key. So, double click the table, here I'm going to

5308
07:51:36,460 --> 07:51:42,520
add a new column, student underline ID, by default we use the int or integer type,

5309
07:51:42,520 --> 07:51:48,358
which allows us up to 2 billion records, and that is pretty sufficient for most

5310
07:51:48,358 --> 07:51:54,123
scenarios. But don't take this as a hard and fast rule, always think about the

5311
07:51:54,123 --> 07:51:54,714
context.

5312
07:51:54,714 --> 07:51:58,579
Alright, now, press tab, let's mark this as a primary key. Now you can see that

5313
07:51:58,579 --> 07:52:02,640
whenever we check this box, not null gets checked by default, because a primary key

5314
07:52:02,640 --> 07:52:06,554
must have a value to identify each record uniquely. Now, by convention we should

5315
07:52:06,554 --> 07:52:10,419
always put the primary key first, this makes it easier for us to understand our

5316
07:52:10,419 --> 07:52:14,480
design. So here on Mac, unfortunately we don't have drag and drop, but we can right

5317
07:52:14,480 --> 07:52:16,828
click this and click move up, let's do it again.

5318
07:52:16,828 --> 07:52:22,672
Unfortunately we don't have drag and drop, but we can right click this and click

5319
07:52:22,672 --> 07:52:28,442
move up, let's do it again, and again, and again. Alright, now we need to add a

5320
07:52:28,442 --> 07:52:34,213
primary key to the courses table. So, once again we cannot rely on any of these

5321
07:52:34,213 --> 07:52:40,349
attributes or columns, so we need to add a new column called course ID. So course ID

5322
07:52:40,349 --> 07:52:46,412
of type integer, and this is going to be a primary key. And by the way, I forgot to

5323
07:52:46,412 --> 07:52:48,238
check this primary key as

5324
07:52:48,238 --> 07:52:54,073
an auto increment column because we want MySQL to automatically generate values for

5325
07:52:54,073 --> 07:52:59,767
this column. This just makes it easier for us to insert records in this table. We

5326
07:52:59,767 --> 07:53:05,673
don't have to worry about the uniqueness of values. Now, let's move this up, so back

5327
07:53:05,673 --> 07:53:11,297
to the students table, here's our student ID column, let's also check this as an

