09:34:45,701 --> 09:34:51,155
storage engine, it used to be popular prior to version 5.5. But going forward,

6559
09:34:51,155 --> 09:34:55,880
InnoDB is the superior storage engine. It supports features like transactions, all

6560
09:34:55,880 --> 09:35:00,663
these things we have talked about so far, these are part of InnoDB. Myisam does not

6561
09:35:00,663 --> 09:35:05,100
support these features. So you really don't want to use Myisam. But there are

6562
09:35:05,100 --> 09:35:09,941
situations where you're working with a database built with an older version of MySQL

6563
09:35:09,941 --> 09:35:14,781
and you may want to upgrade that database to use InnoDB so you can add foreign keys.

6564
09:35:14,781 --> 09:35:17,605
Foreign keys are really important for maintaining

6565
09:35:17,605 --> 09:35:22,166
referential integrity. Earlier I told you that when we add a relationship between

6566
09:35:22,166 --> 09:35:26,839
two tables, we cannot add invalid values in our foreign key columns. So that is the

6567
09:35:26,839 --> 09:35:31,400
benefit of foreign keys. Now let me show you how to change the storage engine. We

6568
09:35:31,400 --> 09:35:36,130
set the storage engine at a table level, so in the same database we can use multiple

6569
09:35:36,130 --> 09:35:40,015
storage engines. For example, here I've got the customers table open,

6570
09:35:40,015 --> 09:35:45,633
here on the top we have this drop down list for selecting the storage engine. As you

6571
09:35:45,633 --> 09:35:51,050
can see, InnoDB is the default storage engine, and we really don't want to change

6572
09:35:51,050 --> 09:35:56,534
this to anything else. But if this table was built with an older version of MySQL,

6573
09:35:56,534 --> 09:36:02,085
perhaps here we would have my ISAM, we could simply change it to InnoDB right here.

6574
09:36:02,085 --> 09:36:07,636
But we can also do the same in SQL. So, back to our query window, we can type alter

6575
09:36:07,636 --> 09:36:09,575
table customers engine equals

6576
09:36:09,575 --> 09:36:14,395
inodb. So this is how we change the storage engine for a table. Just remember that

6577
09:36:14,395 --> 09:36:19,273
changing the storage engine of a table can be an expensive operation, because MySQL

6578
09:36:19,273 --> 09:36:23,564
has to rebuild the table. During this time, your table is not going to be

6579
09:36:23,564 --> 09:36:28,442
accessible. So don't do this in production unless you have a scheduled maintenance.

6580
09:36:28,442 --> 09:36:33,379
So that's all about storage engines that you need to know, and that brings us to the

6581
09:36:33,379 --> 09:36:37,905
end of this section. I hope you learned a lot, and I will see you in the next

6582
09:36:37,905 --> 09:36:38,375
section.

6583
09:36:40,575 --> 09:36:44,986
In this section we're going to look at indexing for high performance. Indexes are

6584
09:36:44,986 --> 09:36:49,397
extremely important in large databases and high traffic websites because they can

6585
09:36:49,397 --> 09:36:53,971
improve the performance of our queries dramatically. In this section you're going to

6586
09:36:53,971 --> 09:36:58,382
learn all about indexes, how they work and how we can create them to speed up our

6587
09:36:58,382 --> 09:37:02,358
queries. This is a very important topic that every developer and database

6588
09:37:02,358 --> 09:37:06,769
administrator must learn and understand. Now before we get started, I want you to

6589
09:37:06,769 --> 09:37:10,145
download and execute the script I've attached to this lecture.

6590
09:37:10,145 --> 09:37:15,137
With this script, we're going to populate our customers table with a thousand new

6591
09:37:15,137 --> 09:37:20,252
records so we can see the impact of indexes on our queries. Now, let's get started.

6592
09:37:20,252 --> 09:37:25,120
Indexes are basically data structures that database engines use to quickly find

6593
09:37:25,120 --> 09:37:30,297
data. As an analogy, think of a telephone directory. Using a telephone directory, we

6594
09:37:30,297 --> 09:37:35,288
can quickly find someone's phone number because contacts are sorted by their last

6595
09:37:35,288 --> 09:37:39,849
and first names. This way we don't have to go through every contact in the

6596
09:37:39,849 --> 09:37:40,465
directory.

6597
09:37:40,465 --> 09:37:45,331
Database management systems use indexes to find data in a similar way. Let's say we

6598
09:37:45,331 --> 09:37:50,079
want to find customers located in California. Without an index, MySQL has to scan

6599
09:37:50,079 --> 09:37:55,004
every record in the customer's table. This is not a big deal in a small table with a

6600
09:37:55,004 --> 09:37:59,694
few hundred or thousand records, but as our tables grow larger, the cost of this

6601
09:37:59,694 --> 09:38:04,501
query is going to increase dramatically. We can speed up this query by creating an

6602
09:38:04,501 --> 09:38:09,426
index on the state column, and this is like creating a directory of customers sorted

6603
09:38:09,426 --> 09:38:10,305
by their state.

6604
09:38:10,305 --> 09:38:15,263
In this directory, or more accurately, this index, we only have the state of our

6605
09:38:15,263 --> 09:38:20,470
customers and references to the records in the customers table. So MySQL can quickly

6606
09:38:20,470 --> 09:38:25,304
find the corresponding customers using this index, and then it will read those

6607
09:38:25,304 --> 09:38:30,262
records from the table. This is way faster than scanning or reading every single

6608
09:38:30,262 --> 09:38:35,469
record in the customers table. In a lot of cases, indexes are small enough that they

6609
09:38:35,469 --> 09:38:40,055
can fit into memory. That's why it's much faster to use them to find data.

6610
09:38:40,055 --> 09:38:45,149
Because reading data from the memory is always faster than reading it from the disk.

6611
09:38:45,149 --> 09:38:50,181
So indexes help us find data quickly, but they come with a cost. First of all, they

6612
09:38:50,181 --> 09:38:55,154
increase the size of our databases because they have to be permanently stored next

6613
09:38:55,154 --> 09:39:00,005
to our tables. Also, every time we add, update, or delete a record, MySQL has to

6614
09:39:00,005 --> 09:39:04,977
update the corresponding indexes and this will impact the performance of our write

6615
09:39:04,977 --> 09:39:08,615
operations. For these reasons, we should reserve indexes for

6616
09:39:08,615 --> 09:39:13,374
performance critical queries. One of the common mistakes that a lot of developers

6617
09:39:13,374 --> 09:39:17,957
make is that they add indexes at the time of designing their tables. You don't

6618
09:39:17,957 --> 09:39:22,833
create indexes based on your tables, you create them based on your queries, because

6619
09:39:22,833 --> 09:39:27,768
the whole point of using an index is to speed up a slow query. Adding an index based

6620
09:39:27,768 --> 09:39:32,469
on the design of a table is like solving a problem that doesn't exist. So, don't

6621
09:39:32,469 --> 09:39:37,110
blindly add indexes on your tables, because this will increase the size of your

6622
09:39:37,110 --> 09:39:37,815
database and

6623
09:39:37,815 --> 09:39:42,382
slow down your writes. Now, internally, indexes are often stored as binary trees,

6624
09:39:42,382 --> 09:39:46,779
but in this course, I'm going to present them as tables because it's easier to

6625
09:39:46,779 --> 09:39:51,120
visualize and understand them. In case you're not familiar with binary trees,

6626
09:39:51,120 --> 09:39:55,518
they're basically one of the fundamental data structures that computer science

6627
09:39:55,518 --> 09:40:00,197
students learn at school. You don't really need to understand how binary trees work

6628
09:40:00,197 --> 09:40:04,707
to work with database indexes. So, for simplicity, think of indexes as tables as

6629
09:40:04,707 --> 09:40:06,455
I've presented in this lecture.

6630
09:40:06,455 --> 09:40:13,093
Now let's see how we can create an index. Following the example from the last

6631
09:40:13,093 --> 09:40:20,335
lecture, let's write a query to get the customers located in California. So, select,

6632
09:40:20,335 --> 09:40:27,405
for now let's only pick the customer ID column, don't use a star, we'll talk about

6633
09:40:27,405 --> 09:40:34,646
this later. So, customer ID from the customers table, where state equals California.

6634
09:40:34,646 --> 09:40:40,595
When we execute this, obviously we see these numbers here. Beautiful.

6635
09:40:40,595 --> 09:40:45,342
Now let me show you how MySQL actually executes this query. So we can prefix this

6636
09:40:45,342 --> 09:40:50,088
with the explain keyword, and now when we run this, instead of seeing this actual

6637
09:40:50,088 --> 09:40:54,777
result, we're going to see something else. Take a look. So we have these columns

6638
09:40:54,777 --> 09:40:59,699
here like select type, table, partitions, type, and so on. Now don't worry about all

6639
09:40:59,699 --> 09:41:04,504
these columns, you're going to learn about them as we go through this section. The

6640
09:41:04,504 --> 09:41:08,255
two columns that you need to pay attention to are type and rows.

6641
09:41:08,255 --> 09:41:14,290
When we see all under type, that means MySQL is going to do a full table scan, which

6642
09:41:14,290 --> 09:41:20,324
means it's going to read or scan every single record in this table. Here in the rows

6643
09:41:20,324 --> 09:41:26,144
column, you can see the number of records that were scanned. In this case we have

6644
09:41:26,144 --> 09:41:31,963
1010, because that's the number of records we have in the customers table. Take a

6645
09:41:31,963 --> 09:41:37,710
look. So, select count of everything from the customers table. There you go. So,

6646
09:41:37,710 --> 09:41:39,075
back to this query.

6647
09:41:39,555 --> 09:41:44,690
Because currently we don't have an index on the state column, in order to find the

6648
09:41:44,690 --> 09:41:49,574
customers located in California, MySQL has to scan every single record in this

6649
09:41:49,574 --> 09:41:54,584
table. And this can get very slow as our customers table grows large. What if we

6650
09:41:54,584 --> 09:41:59,844
have a million records here? This query is going to take several seconds or minutes.

6651
09:41:59,844 --> 09:42:04,916
This is where we use an index. So we put an index on the state column to speed up

6652
09:42:04,916 --> 09:42:08,485
this query. Let me show you. So, we type out create index

6653
09:42:08,485 --> 09:42:14,387
We give our index a name, quite often we prefix the name by idx or ix. Then we add

6654
09:42:14,387 --> 09:42:20,290
an underline and here we specify the name of the column that we're going to put an

6655
09:42:20,290 --> 09:42:26,192
index on. In this case, state. As a best practice, always give meaningful names to

6656
09:42:26,192 --> 09:42:32,238
our indexes. I've seen people creating indexes like index 1, index 2, what does this

6657
09:42:32,238 --> 09:42:36,485
represent? I have no idea. So, always use meaningful names.

6658
09:42:36,615 --> 09:42:42,592
So here we create an index called idx state on customers table, and inside this

6659
09:42:42,592 --> 09:42:48,871
parenthesis we type out the name of the columns we want to put an index on, in this

6660
09:42:48,871 --> 09:42:54,847
case state. So, let's execute this line, beautiful. Now let's execute the first

6661
09:42:54,847 --> 09:43:00,824
statement one more time, take a look. In the type column we no longer have all,

6662
09:43:00,824 --> 09:43:06,725
which means we don't have a full table scan, and in the rows column we see 112

6663
09:43:06,725 --> 09:43:12,364
So we reduce the number of records that MySQL has to read from 1010 to 112. This is

6664
09:43:12,364 --> 09:43:17,934
far faster. Now look under the possible keys. These are various indexes that MySQL

6665
09:43:17,934 --> 09:43:23,573
might consider for executing this query. It is possible that we might have multiple

6666
09:43:23,573 --> 09:43:29,143
indexes, so MySQL picks the one that has the best performance. So here we have the

6667
09:43:29,143 --> 09:43:34,714
possible keys or indexes, and in the key column we can see the actual index or key

6668
09:43:34,714 --> 09:43:35,665
that was used.

6669
09:43:35,665 --> 09:43:42,661
So this is how an index can speed up our query. Now it's time for an exercise. Write

6670
09:43:42,661 --> 09:43:48,908
a query to find customers with more than a thousand points. Use the explain

6671
09:43:48,908 --> 09:43:55,904
statement to see how many records MySQL is going to scan to produce the result. Then

6672
09:43:55,904 --> 09:44:02,484
create an index on this column and compare the result. You will see my solution

6673
09:44:02,484 --> 09:44:09,063
next. Alright, so first let's select customer ID from the customers table where

6674
09:44:09,063 --> 09:44:11,895
points is greater than a thousand.

6675
09:44:11,895 --> 09:44:17,945
Now we're going to use the explain keyword to see how MySQL executes this query. So

6676
09:44:17,945 --> 09:44:23,995
as you can see we have a full table scanned and the number of records that MySQL is

6677
09:44:23,995 --> 09:44:29,971
going to read is 1010. So, let's go ahead and create an index on the points column

6678
09:44:29,971 --> 09:44:36,021
so we can quickly find customers with more than a thousand points. Create index idx

6679
09:44:36,021 --> 09:44:41,415
underline points on customers table and in parenthesis we type out points.

6680
09:44:42,115 --> 09:44:47,151
Let's execute this line, beautiful. Now we're going to explain this query one more

6681
09:44:47,151 --> 09:44:52,249
time. This time we have range instead of all, which means we're looking for a range

6682
09:44:52,249 --> 09:44:57,408
of records. Under possible keys we can see the index that we just created. Obviously

6683
09:44:57,408 --> 09:45:02,261
we can see that MySQL picked this index and here are the number of records that

6684
09:45:02,261 --> 09:45:07,235
MySQL had to read. So we reduced the number of records from 1000 to 500. Next I'm

6685
09:45:07,235 --> 09:45:10,245
going to show you how to view indexes on a table.

6686
09:45:15,745 --> 09:45:21,120
Alright, now to view the indexes on a table, we type show indexes in, and here we

6687
09:45:21,120 --> 09:45:26,562
type the name of our table. Customers. Take a look. So here we have three indexes,

6688
09:45:26,562 --> 09:45:31,671
the first one is the primary key, this is also called the clustered index, so

6689
09:45:31,671 --> 09:45:37,113
whenever we add a primary key to a table, MySQL automatically creates an index, so

6690
09:45:37,113 --> 09:45:42,355
we can quickly look up records by their ID. Here you can see that this index is

6691
09:45:42,355 --> 09:45:44,545
placed on the customer ID column.

6692
09:45:44,545 --> 09:45:50,021
Collation represents how data is sorted in the index. A means ascending, we also

6693
09:45:50,021 --> 09:45:55,703
have B which means descending. Cardinality represents an estimated number of unique

6694
09:45:55,703 --> 09:46:01,316
values in the index. So the cardinality of this primary key index is 1010, because

6695
09:46:01,316 --> 09:46:07,066
we have 1010 unique values in this column. But take into account that this number is

6696
09:46:07,066 --> 09:46:12,680
an estimate, not an actual value. To get a more accurate value, we use the analyze

6697
09:46:12,680 --> 09:46:13,775
table statement.

6698
09:46:13,775 --> 09:46:19,758
So, we type analyze table customers and this will regenerate the statistics for this

6699
09:46:19,758 --> 09:46:25,669
table. So, let's execute this, alright, now let's look at the indexes in this table

6700
09:46:25,669 --> 09:46:31,580
one more time. So now we should have more accurate values. So one more time here we

6701
09:46:31,580 --> 09:46:37,065
have three indexes, the first one is the primary key which is also called the

6702
09:46:37,065 --> 09:46:42,335
clustered index, so every table can have a maximum of one clustered index.

6703
09:46:42,335 --> 09:46:47,247
Here we have two other indexes, these are referred to as secondary indexes. Now

6704
09:46:47,247 --> 09:46:52,345
technically whenever we create a secondary index, MySQL automatically includes the

6705
09:46:52,345 --> 09:46:57,319
ID or the primary key column in the secondary index. For example, here we have a

6706
09:46:57,319 --> 09:47:02,356
secondary index on the points column, but inside this index we have two values in

6707
09:47:02,356 --> 09:47:07,578
each entry. We have the points as well as the ID for each customer. We don't see the

6708
09:47:07,578 --> 09:47:10,625
ID here, but internally it's stored in the index.

6709
09:47:10,945 --> 09:47:16,441
Now, look at the type of these indexes. All these indexes are Btree, which is short

6710
09:47:16,441 --> 09:47:21,870
for binary tree. That is why earlier I told you that in MySQL, most of our indexes

6711
09:47:21,870 --> 09:47:27,300
are stored as binary trees, but in this course I represent them visually as tables

6712
09:47:27,300 --> 09:47:32,862
because they're easier to understand and reason about. Now let's look at the indexes

6713
09:47:32,862 --> 09:47:38,225
in the orders table. So, show indexes in orders. This table has four indexes, the

6714
09:47:38,225 --> 09:47:40,145
first one is the primary key.

6715
09:47:40,145 --> 09:47:45,520
These three other indexes are the secondary indexes that are placed on our foreign

6716
09:47:45,520 --> 09:47:50,436
key columns. So whenever we create a relationship between two tables, MySQL

6717
09:47:50,436 --> 09:47:55,548
automatically creates an index on the foreign keys, so we can quickly join our

6718
09:47:55,548 --> 09:48:00,989
tables. Alright, so this is how we can see the indexes in a given table, but we can

6719
09:48:00,989 --> 09:48:06,429
also find them in the navigator panel. For example, in our SQL store database, look

6720
09:48:06,429 --> 09:48:10,755
at the customers table, here we have a folder for the indexes, and

6721
09:48:10,755 --> 09:48:16,383
If you click on an index, you can see various attributes about that index. For

6722
09:48:16,383 --> 09:48:22,300
example, this index is a binary tree, it's not unique because we can have multiple

6723
09:48:22,300 --> 09:48:28,217
customers in the same state. It's visible which means it's enabled and it's placed

6724
09:48:28,217 --> 09:48:34,206
on the state column. Next we're going to talk about indexing string columns. So you

6725
09:48:34,206 --> 09:48:40,195
have learned how to create and view indexes. Now if the column we want to create an

6726
09:48:40,195 --> 09:48:44,885
index on is a string column, like a char, varchar, text, or blob,

6727
09:48:44,885 --> 09:48:49,947
Our index may consume a lot of disk space and it won't perform well. Smaller indexes

6728
09:48:49,947 --> 09:48:54,768
are better because they can fit in memory and this makes our searches faster. So

6729
09:48:54,768 --> 09:48:59,529
when indexing string columns, we don't want to include the entire column in the

6730
09:48:59,529 --> 09:49:04,531
index, we only want to include the first few characters or the prefix of the column

6731
09:49:04,531 --> 09:49:09,593
so our index will be smaller. Let me show you. Let's say you want to create an index

6732
09:49:09,593 --> 09:49:14,655
on the last name column in the customers table. So create index idx on the line last

6733
09:49:14,655 --> 09:49:20,486
name, on customers, on the last name column. Now because the last name column is a

6734
09:49:20,486 --> 09:49:26,317
string column, here in parenthesis we can specify the number of characters we want

6735
09:49:26,317 --> 09:49:32,005
to include in the index. This is optional for char and varchar columns, but it's

6736
09:49:32,005 --> 09:49:37,907
compulsory for text and blob columns. So here let's say we want to include only the

6737
09:49:37,907 --> 09:49:42,245
first 20 characters in the index. Now we have a prefix index.

6738
09:49:42,245 --> 09:49:47,115
You may be wondering why I chose 20 here. Is it a magic number or a best practice?

6739
09:49:47,115 --> 09:49:51,808
Not really. To find the optimal number, we have to look at our data. We want to

6740
09:49:51,808 --> 09:49:56,737
include enough characters that can uniquely identify each customer. For example, if

6741
09:49:56,737 --> 09:50:01,667
we include only one character, we'll have a lot of customers whose last names start

6742
09:50:01,667 --> 09:50:06,656
with A or B or C. This is not a good index because it doesn't allow MySQL to quickly

6743
09:50:06,656 --> 09:50:08,735
find a customer by their last name.

6744
09:50:08,735 --> 09:50:14,374
The index may return 100,000 customers whose last names start with A. Then my skill

6745
09:50:14,374 --> 09:50:19,741
has to go through all these 100,000 customers to find someone with a given last

6746
09:50:19,741 --> 09:50:25,448
name. So let me show you how to find the optimal prefix length. First, let's get the

6747
09:50:25,448 --> 09:50:30,951
number of records in this table. So select count of everything from the customers

6748
09:50:30,951 --> 09:50:36,454
table. We have 1010 records. Now let's try a few different prefix lengths and see

6749
09:50:36,454 --> 09:50:39,375
how many unique values we get. For example,

6750
09:50:39,375 --> 09:50:45,469
Let's get the first character of our customer's last names. So we type left of last

6751
09:50:45,469 --> 09:50:51,564
name and we only want to get one character. Now this will return a lot of duplicate

6752
09:50:51,564 --> 09:50:57,658
values like a, a, a, b, b, b and so on. So we apply the distinct keyword to see how

6753
09:50:57,658 --> 09:51:03,679
many unique values we get. Take a look. So we have 25 unique values. Our goal here

6754
09:51:03,679 --> 09:51:08,305
should be to maximize the number of unique values in our index.

6755
09:51:08,305 --> 09:51:13,139
So let's try a few different lengths. I'm going to put this on a new line so we can

6756
09:51:13,139 --> 09:51:14,653
compare them side by side.

6757
09:51:14,653 --> 09:51:21,884
Let's put from here as well. Now, let's duplicate this line and try a different

6758
09:51:21,884 --> 09:51:29,389
prefix, let's say the first 5 characters. With this we get 966 unique values. This

6759
09:51:29,389 --> 09:51:36,619
is pretty good since we have 1010 records in this table. What if we include the

6760
09:51:36,619 --> 09:51:44,307
first 10 characters? So 10, alright we see a very small improvement, so we only have

6761
09:51:44,307 --> 09:51:47,053
30 more unique values compared

6762
09:51:47,053 --> 09:51:52,422
