customer id from customers, order by state.

7172
10:26:56,081 --> 10:27:01,659
Let's explain this query and see what we get. So here we have an index scan and if

7173
10:27:01,659 --> 10:27:07,305
you look at the extra column you can see that MySQL is satisfying this query purely

7174
10:27:07,305 --> 10:27:12,951
using our index. Now let's see what happens if we select all columns. We get a full

7175
10:27:12,951 --> 10:27:18,460
table scan. And as you know this can be very expensive. So here's the reason. The

7176
10:27:18,460 --> 10:27:24,174
composite index that we put on the state and points columns contains three pieces of

7177
10:27:24,174 --> 10:27:26,351
information about each customer.

7178
10:27:26,351 --> 10:27:32,465
Their ID, their state, and their points. Just to refresh your memory, earlier I told

7179
10:27:32,465 --> 10:27:38,288
you that whenever you create a secondary index, MySQL automatically includes the

7180
10:27:38,288 --> 10:27:44,257
primary key in your secondary index. So that's why our index contains the customer

7181
10:27:44,257 --> 10:27:50,153
ID, state, and points. So, if we pick any of these columns here, like customer ID

7182
10:27:50,153 --> 10:27:54,811
and state, MySQL can satisfy our query entirely using our index.

7183
10:27:54,811 --> 10:27:59,729
This is called a covering index. An index that covers everything that a query needs.

7184
10:27:59,729 --> 10:28:04,412
So using this index, MySQL can execute our query without touching the table. And

7185
10:28:04,412 --> 10:28:09,213
this is the fastest performance we can get. So when designing your indexes, first,

7186
10:28:09,213 --> 10:28:13,780
look at your where clause. Look at the columns that are most frequently there.

7187
10:28:13,780 --> 10:28:18,580
Include those in your index. With this you can narrow down the searches. Then look

7188
10:28:18,580 --> 10:28:23,440
at the columns in the order by clause. See if you can include these columns in your

7189
10:28:23,440 --> 10:28:23,791
index.

7190
10:28:23,791 --> 10:28:29,239
And finally look at the columns used in the select clause. If you include these

7191
10:28:29,239 --> 10:28:34,964
columns as well, then you will get a covering index, so MySQL can use your index to

7192
10:28:34,964 --> 10:28:40,412
satisfy your query. So, indexes can dramatically improve the performance of our

7193
10:28:40,412 --> 10:28:45,722
queries. But too much of a good thing can be a bad thing. So as you work with

7194
10:28:45,722 --> 10:28:51,309
indexes, you need to watch for duplicate and redundant indexes. Duplicate indexes

7195
10:28:51,309 --> 10:28:56,481
are indexes on the same set of columns in the same order, like ABC and ABC.

7196
10:28:56,481 --> 10:29:01,159
The problem is that MySQL doesn't stop you from creating these duplicate indexes. It

7197
10:29:01,159 --> 10:29:05,670
will maintain each duplicate index separately. Sometimes you can create duplicate

7198
10:29:05,670 --> 10:29:10,292
indexes without being aware of it. This often happens when someone creates an index

7199
10:29:10,292 --> 10:29:14,747
without looking at the existing indexes. So as a best practice, always check the

7200
10:29:14,747 --> 10:29:19,091
existing indexes before creating a new one. Redundant indexes are a little bit

7201
10:29:19,091 --> 10:29:23,769
different. If you have an index on two columns like A and B, and then create another

7202
10:29:23,769 --> 10:29:24,771
index on column A,

7203
10:29:24,771 --> 10:29:29,540
that is considered redundant. Because the former index can also optimize the queries

7204
10:29:29,540 --> 10:29:34,139
that worked with column A. However, if you create an index on columns B and A, or

7205
10:29:34,139 --> 10:29:38,511
just column B, that is not redundant, because these indexes satisfy different

7206
10:29:38,511 --> 10:29:43,109
queries. Again, redundant indexes appear when someone creates a new index without

7207
10:29:43,109 --> 10:29:47,765
looking at the existing indexes. So, always, always, always, before creating a new

7208
10:29:47,765 --> 10:29:52,194
index, look at the existing indexes, and see if there is an index that you can

7209
10:29:52,194 --> 10:29:52,591
extend.

7210
10:29:52,591 --> 10:29:58,102
So index maintenance is very important. Make sure to drop, duplicate, redundant, as

